<!--
  Production overlay for ClickHouse memory and concurrency caps.

  Mounted by docker-compose.prod.yml at:
    /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/memory_limits.xml

  Why this exists:
    config.xml ships with max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio=0.9, which on
    a 30 GB single-host install lets ClickHouse grab ~27 GB. That leaves
    nothing for Postgres, Kafka, Django/Node services, and triggers cascading
    OOM-kills under any meaningful load.

  Settings here override config.xml for prod. Tune for your host:

    max_server_memory_usage   - hard cap on RSS (in bytes). 8 GB is fine for
                                a single-tenant hobby/mobile install with a
                                few hundred MB of events. Bump to 12-16 GB
                                if you have >32 GB host RAM.
    max_thread_pool_size      - context-switch budget. Default 10000 is sized
                                for 64-core servers; 1000 is plenty for 8 cores.
    max_concurrent_queries    - global concurrency limit. Single-user installs
                                rarely exceed 5 simultaneous queries; 50 is
                                generous.
    mark_cache_size           - cached primary-key marks (default 5 GB).
                                1 GB is enough for hobby data volumes.
    uncompressed_cache_size   - cached uncompressed blocks (default 8 GB).
                                Most queries don't benefit; 1 GB is fine.

  Per-query memory cap is in users.d.prod/memory_per_query.xml (3 GB), which
  overrides the 10 GB default from users.xml. Server cap (8 GB here) must be
  >= per-query cap × N for the largest realistic concurrent N. With 3 GB
  per-query and 8 GB server, ~2-3 heavy queries fit simultaneously; queries
  beyond that fail with "Memory limit (total) for all queries exceeded" —
  recoverable, the server stays alive.

  If max_concurrent_queries × per-query > server cap (this config: 50 × 3 = 150 GB
  vs 8 GB server), ClickHouse uses memory_overcommit to cancel the largest
  in-progress query when the server cap is hit. Server stays alive either way.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <max_server_memory_usage>8589934592</max_server_memory_usage>             <!-- 8 GB absolute cap -->
    <!--
      Active cap = min(max_server_memory_usage, ratio × cgroup_RAM).
      "available RAM" from CH's POV is the CONTAINER's cgroup limit
      (x-mem-clickhouse in docker-compose.prod.yml), NOT the host RAM.
      Currently 14 GB cgroup × 0.5 ratio = 7 GB CH cap. The 8 GB absolute
      kicks in only if the cgroup grows to >16 GB.

      Tuning rule: pick `mem-clickhouse` ≤ ~half of host RAM, then ratio
      so that `cgroup × ratio` is ~70-80% of cgroup. The 0.3 default was
      sized for an unrelated install and was way too tight after the
      events-export workload landed (Code 241 OOM at 3 GB cap).

      If you bump x-mem-clickhouse, optionally bump the absolute cap above
      proportionally so this knob remains the operative one.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.5</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>
    <max_thread_pool_size>1000</max_thread_pool_size>
    <max_concurrent_queries>50</max_concurrent_queries>
    <mark_cache_size>1073741824</mark_cache_size>                             <!-- 1 GB -->
    <uncompressed_cache_size>1073741824</uncompressed_cache_size>             <!-- 1 GB -->
</clickhouse>
